<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <RootNamespace>Ruledger.Cli</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>False</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1605</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Ruledger from a shell. The walk, the test design and the diff are the Ruledger library's,
    published as its own package; this tool is one host of it, and it prints what the library
    answers. The package carries the .Cli suffix the ecosystem uses, as Rulealize.Cli does
    beside Rulealize.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>ruledger</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>Ruledger.Cli</PackageId>
    <Version>1.3.0</Version>
    <Authors>Reny</Authors>
    <Description>Ruledger from a command line: walks a Rulealize rule set and writes down the test design — for every state it visits, which inputs are legal, whether it is final and with what result, and where each legal input leads — and applies one to a later version of the rule set, which is the diff, carrying the choices a person made across it.</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/reny-develop/Ruledger.Cli</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The walk, the test design and the diff. Nothing of them is written here: a second account of
    what a test design says would be one that could disagree with the one a host reads.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Ruledger" Version="1.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The runtime, for the vocabularies a folder holds and for what a document says it holds.
    Reading `uses` through Rulealize rather than out of the JSON here is the same decision
    Rulealize.Cli made: a second reading of it would eventually disagree with the one that
    compiles the document, and the way it would show is this tool handing over a set of
    components the runtime then refuses.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Rulealize" Version="0.11.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    So that what the tool prints can be held against what the documents say it prints. A
    command is a class here rather than a script for that reason: the exit code and the
    lines are the whole of the interface, and both are checked.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Ruledger.Cli.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
